}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "In the decoder’s self‑attention layer a mask is applied so that each position can only attend to earlier positions in the output sequence. The mask blocks attention scores for any “future” token (i.e., positions > i) and thus prevents the model from using information that has not yet been generated [attention_is_all_you_need_v7.pdf, p. 3].

Because the output embeddings are shifted by one position, this masked self‑attention guarantees that the prediction for position i can depend solely on the already‑produced tokens at positions < i, enforcing an autoregressive decoding process [attention_is_all_you_need_v7.pdf, p. 3].", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1446.026479000011, "tokens_in": 1652, "tokens_out": 292, "total_tokens": 1944}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1578, "tokens_out": 375, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "In scaled dot‑product attention each query vector is compared with every key vector by taking their dot product; the resulting scores are divided by √dₖ to temper their magnitude and then passed through a softmax to produce a set of attention weights [attention_is_all_you_need_v7.pdf, p. 4].  

These weights are used to form a weighted sum of the corresponding value vectors, yielding the attention output for that query; in matrix form the operation is Attention(Q,K,V)=softmax(QKᵀ/√dₖ) V [attention_is_all_you_need_v7.pdf, p. 4].", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1551.381111000012, "tokens_in": 1512, "tokens_out": 305, "total_tokens": 1817}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1428, "tokens_out": 376, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "Moshi is designed as a speech‑text foundation model that can produce real‑time dialogue, generating both the user’s and the system’s utterances so that conversations can be created without a live interlocutor【moshi_v1.pdf, p. 30】.  

Its primary goal is to learn natural conversational dynamics—such as turn‑taking, pauses, and overlaps—by training a multi‑stream architecture that models the flow of spoken interaction in real time【moshi_v1.pdf, p. 30】.", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1247.256778999997, "tokens_in": 1751, "tokens_out": 280, "total_tokens": 2031}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1642, "tokens_out": 408, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "Moshi enables real‑time spoken dialogue by treating conversation as speech‑to‑speech generation rather than a cascade of separate modules.  It runs a multi‑stream Transformer that models the system’s speech and the user’s speech in parallel token streams, removing the need for explicit speaker‑turn segmentation and allowing overlapping speech, interruptions and interjections to be handled continuously.  This full‑duplex design yields a theoretical latency of 160 ms and about 200 ms in practice, making interaction comparable to a natural conversation【moshi_v1.pdf, p. 1】.  

The model generates tokens hierarchically in a streaming fashion: a text language‑model backbone (Helium) first predicts time‑aligned text tokens (“Inner Monologue”), which are then used as a prefix for semantic and acoustic tokens produced by a residual‑vector‑quantized neural audio codec (Mimi).  This simultaneous production of text, semantic and acoustic tokens provides streaming speech recognition and text‑to‑speech, while also allowing the synthesis of varied emotions, voices and acoustic conditions in real time【moshi_v1.pdf, p. 6】.", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1694.4225679981173, "tokens_in": 1495, "tokens_out": 623, "total_tokens": 2118}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1513, "tokens_out": 547, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "Mimi is the neural audio codec integrated into Moshi that tokenizes speech into semantic and acoustic representations. The codec’s semantic VQ tokens are evaluated for phonetic discriminability and are used as targets for language‑modeling, while its acoustic tokens enable high‑quality reconstruction of the waveform【moshi_v1.pdf, p. 23】.  

In addition to tokenization, Mimi functions as Moshi’s compression‑decompression engine. The system uses the Mimi auto‑encoder to compress and later reconstruct generated speech, a step that is explicitly evaluated in the watermarking experiments【moshi_v1.pdf, p. 39】.", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1311.455200004275, "tokens_in": 1814, "tokens_out": 421, "total_tokens": 2235}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1733, "tokens_out": 465, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "Streaming is a fundamental part of Moshi’s design, allowing the model to generate semantic and acoustic tokens hierarchically for both the user and the system while keeping text tokens time‑aligned when using the Inner Monologue mechanism【moshi_v1.pdf, p. 6】.  

By adjusting the delay between text and audio tokens, Moshi can operate as a streaming ASR or streaming TTS system: a 2‑second look‑ahead is used for audio tokens in TTS and for text tokens in ASR, enabling the model to listen to incoming speech before producing output and thus supporting real‑time dialogue with low latency【moshi_v1.pdf, p. 31】.", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1407.1727159971488, "tokens_in": 1560, "tokens_out": 439, "total_tokens": 1999}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1487, "tokens_out": 650, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "Dynamic chunking is a learned, data‑dependent subsampling mechanism that compresses input sequences in a content‑aware way by optimizing the chunk boundaries end‑to‑end together with the rest of the model [dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 4].  

In hierarchical sequence modeling, this mechanism enables the network to build higher‑level abstractions from low‑level data—effectively “chunking” tokens into larger semantic units—so that each hierarchical stage processes a progressively compressed representation, improving compute efficiency and reasoning over the sequence [dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 4].", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1385.0459889945341, "tokens_in": 1405, "tokens_out": 362, "total_tokens": 1767}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1313, "tokens_out": 395, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "Dynamic chunking selects chunk boundaries adaptively, letting the model retain vectors that are semantically important rather than following a preset pattern; this content‑adaptive compression lets the system learn which parts of the sequence to keep based on meaning [dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 8].

In contrast, fixed‑size (static) chunking applies uniform, predetermined windows that ignore natural semantic boundaries, often cutting words or phrases arbitrarily and limiting model expressivity [dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 32].", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1160.1402960004634, "tokens_in": 1483, "tokens_out": 291, "total_tokens": 1774}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1374, "tokens_out": 360, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "The proposed method builds a hierarchical network (H‑Net) that recursively compresses raw inputs through a **data‑dependent dynamic chunking** process. Raw data are first encoded by a small encoder, which creates subsampled sequences that are then fed to a more powerful language model, yielding an autoregressive U‑Net‑like hierarchy that captures multiple resolution levels without relying on handcrafted tokenizers【dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 2】.  

To process the higher‑level stages, the architecture incorporates **Mamba (‑2) layers** in the outer parts of the hierarchy, providing linear‑time efficiency and superior modeling of high‑resolution signals such as characters and DNA bases. This contrasts with static chunking approaches that use fixed‑size intervals and simple pooling, which the method replaces with learned, context‑aware segmentation【dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 31】.", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1515.9195820015157, "tokens_in": 1442, "tokens_out": 483, "total_tokens": 1925}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1409, "tokens_out": 599, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

[{"attempt": 1, "candidate_answer": "Dynamic chunking lets an end‑to‑end hierarchical network compress raw inputs while keeping the same computational cost as traditional tokenized pipelines, yet it yields substantially better modeling ability by replacing fixed pooling heuristics with content‑aware, context‑dependent segmentation learned directly from data【dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 2】.  

The mechanism consists of a routing module that predicts boundary scores and a smoothing module that interpolates representations based on those scores; this combination dampens the impact of uncertain boundaries and markedly improves the learnability and stability of the model during training【dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 2】.  

Because the whole system is differentiable, the chunking process can be applied recursively—each main network can itself be an H‑Net—so that successive stages create increasingly abstract representations (e.g., characters → words → clauses), which broadens the benefits of chunking to general sequence‑modeling tasks across many modalities【dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 4】.", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1522.9458969988627, "tokens_in": 1426, "tokens_out": 484, "total_tokens": 1910}, "verification": {"verified": false, "reason": "One factual claim about stability is not supported by the provided passages.", "tokens_in": 1417, "tokens_out": 669, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": false,
  "reason": "One factual claim about stability is not supported by the provided passages.",
  "unsupported_claims": [
    "The claim that the smoothing module \"markedly improves the ... stability of the model during training\" is not mentioned in the supplied text."
  ]
}"}}, {"attempt": 2, "candidate_answer": "Dynamic chunking lets an end‑to‑end hierarchical network compress raw inputs with a data‑dependent segmentation that matches the computational efficiency of traditional tokenized pipelines while substantially improving modeling ability, replacing handcrafted heuristics with learned, content‑aware boundaries【dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 2】.  

The mechanism combines a routing module that predicts boundaries via similarity scores with a smoothing module that interpolates representations, which attenuates the impact of uncertain boundaries and markedly enhances learnability【dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 2】.  

Because the architecture is fully end‑to‑end, the chunking process can be applied recursively, enabling multiple stages of abstraction (e.g., characters → words → clauses) and yielding better general‑purpose sequence modeling across a wide range of modalities【dynamic_chunking_v2.pdf, p. 4】.", "generation": {"model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "finish_reason": "stop", "latency_ms": 1615.4433910051011, "tokens_in": 1493, "tokens_out": 535, "total_tokens": 2028}, "verification": {"verified": true, "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported", "tokens_in": 1386, "tokens_out": 548, "raw_response": "{
  "supported": true,
  "reason": "All factual claims and citations are supported",
  "unsupported_claims": []
}"}}]

